<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Liang</h1><p class="st-meta">907 CE – 923 CE · capital Kaifeng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first of the Five Dynasties, founded by the former rebel Zhu Wen when he ended the Tang in 907, with its capital at Kaifeng.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a>, a lieutenant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a>, defected to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> in 882 and was made governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Bianzhou</a> (Kaifeng). Over two decades he became the strongest warlord in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, took control of the court, killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhaozong-of-tang">Emperor Zhaozong</a> in 904 and in 907 forced the last Tang emperor to abdicate, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-dynasty">Liang dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-liang-555-587">Later Liang</a> held the central plains along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, at the head of the canal system, its capital, a choice later dynasties followed. Its rule rested on a professional army, but it never subdued its rival <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jin-five-dynasties">Jin (Five Dynasties)</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> and lost a major battle at Bochao in 911. Many regional rulers in the south accepted its titles while governing independently.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> was murdered by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-yougui">Zhu Yougui</a> in 912, who was overthrown by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-youzhen">Zhu Youzhen</a> in 913. Jin steadily took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, and in 923 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a>, now ruler of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>; Zhu Youzhen had himself killed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-liang.jpg" alt="Later Liang" width="320" height="437"><figcaption>Image: Pavo Xie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L.LIANG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>907 CE – 923 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kaifeng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhu Wen (Emperor Taizu), Zhu Yougui, Zhu Youzhen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Later Liang (907-923), Liang (Zhu Wen)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=915&amp;focus=later-liang" aria-label="Later Liang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7535 923 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Later Liang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7855 1179L7771 1192M7855 1179L7898 1125M7855 1179L8118 1329M7855 1179L8151 1476M7855 1179L7959 1401"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7771 1192h0M7898 1125h0M8118 1329h0M8151 1476h0M7959 1401h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7855 1179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=915&amp;focus=later-liang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
